WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:06.790
Over at microcosmos.store, you can pick up
your very own Microcosmos Microscope.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:12.210
It comes with four Plan Objectives to ensure
the sharpest possible image and using the

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:16.691
included phone mount, you can capture images
like the ones you’re seeing right now, all

00:00:16.691 --> 00:00:20.090
of which were recorded on our microscope.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:30.000
Check it out today at microcosmos.store

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.290
In the 1820s, a man named Dr. R. Brandes walked
through a meadow on a quest to try and answer

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:45.300
a centuries-old question that had recently
begun to spark discussion among his own correspondences.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.510
Several of his acquaintances had come across
masses of a mysterious gelatinous substance

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:59.200
on the ground known as “star jelly,” but
no one knew what it could possibly be.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:16.030
But on the list of possibilities was this:
a bacteria called nostoc.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:22.420
Our first discoveries of bacteria are generally
credited to Robert Hooke and Antoni van Leeuwenhoek,

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.790
who wielded their microscopes to explore the
invisible worlds embedded in our surroundings

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:33.890
and reported back with descriptions of tiny,
living things.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:39.240
Their work goes back to the 17th century,
relatively recent as far as human endeavors

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.240
go.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:47.970
And yet our knowledge of nostoc is much, much
older, going back at least 1,600 years, when

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:52.490
they featured in Chinese writings describing
their use as food.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:02:00.990
And somehow the bacteria was named “nostoc”
in the 16th century, before we knew that bacteria

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:01.990
existed.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.950
But how?

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.869
How could we know about a bacteria before
we knew their type existed?

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:13.400
How could we see them, let alone eat them
or name them or write about them?

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.340
How could someone like Dr. Brandes possibly
look at a lump of jelly on the ground and

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:25.310
think it has anything to do with the little
dots we need a microscope to see right now?

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:31.730
Well, it’s because nostoc don’t actually
live as a single little set of dots on their

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:32.730
own.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:38.290
They are part of something larger than them,
much much larger.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:45.400
They exist as a colony, a mass of nostoc held
together like these ones.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:52.250
Nostoc are cyanobacteria, and the individual
cells are usually shaped like spheres or ovals.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:58.599
These cells are attached to each other, forming
the short strands we can see here, which are

00:02:58.599 --> 00:02:59.599
called trichomes.

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:04.290
But not all of the cells of the nostoc are
the same.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:09.750
Life in a colony means that different cells
can take on different tasks instead of having

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.790
to try and accomplish everything at once.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:18.629
Some specialize in reproduction, while others
fix nitrogen to help the cells grow.

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:23.840
And some rare cells remain dormant behind
thick walls.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.819
Their main task is survival, ensuring the
nostoc colony can grow from something again

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:35.230
should environmental conditions take a turn
for the worse.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:40.739
There are several hundred species of Nostoc
that we know of, and there is a wide range

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:43.439
of sizes to those colonies.

00:03:43.439 --> 00:03:46.750
Some are only a few millimeters in diameter,
while others—

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.870
like Nostoc pruniforme—

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:54.040
can get up to 22 centimeters in diameter.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:04:01.700
What holds the trichomes together so they
can accumulate into these larger sizes is

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.560
a gelatinous matrix made of sugars.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.189
We’ve actually been staring at this jelly
structure the entire time, but it’s hard

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:12.980
to see because the cells within it are so
still.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.930
The gelatinous shell just melts into the background.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.669
But you can see it here.

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:22.870
Or rather, you see the jelly shell being poked
and prodded at by a creature on the other

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.300
side of it.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.630
That matrix does more than hold the colony
together.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:33.389
It protects it, keeping it safe from solar
radiation and other dangers in the environment.

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:38.740
And as you may have guessed, the gelatinous
structure is the magic behind the mystery

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.070
mucus piles that people have found lying on
the ground.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:48.880
In fact, the name “nostoc” is a round-about
tribute to those squishy lumps.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:55.490
In the 16th century, there was a scientist
and alchemist named Aureolus Philippus Theophrastus

00:04:55.490 --> 00:05:03.560
Bombastus von Hohenheim, though he’s become
known by the much shorter “Paracelsus.”

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:08.760
Paracelsus was particularly fascinated by
folklore, and as he traveled throughout Western

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.200
Europe, he often looked to talk to peasants
and craftsmen and others who might be able

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:19.160
to tell him about things like “witch’s
jelly,” another phrase used to describe

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.160
nostoc.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:26.210
Paracelsus was the one who gave the organism
the name it’s known by today, drawing on

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:30.100
Old English and German words for nostril.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:35.930
The inspiration for choosing a variation on
“nostril” comes, we assume, from the very

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:43.069
booger-like appearance of the nostoc, the
clumpy green masses they form along the ground.

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:50.150
One 17th century writer described nostoc as
“excrement blown from the nostrils of some

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:58.819
rheumatick planet, falling upon spacious plains,
fields and sheep pastures.”

00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:05.080
The fascination with nostoc came because their
lumpy, jelly bodies seemed to come out of

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.949
nowhere, but were often associated with rain
or shooting stars.

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:14.870
It’s why their numerous names include things
like “spittle of the stars” and “star

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.319
slime.”

00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:20.070
We now know why nostoc are capable of such
surprising appearances.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.699
It’s not that they come from nowhere.

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:28.310
Rather they come from a dry, desiccated state
that renders them brittle and dark against

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.479
the ground, and thus easily missed.

00:06:31.479 --> 00:06:35.889
They survive these times thanks in part to
the sugars surrounding them, which keep them

00:06:35.889 --> 00:06:38.229
protected from this dry state.

00:06:38.229 --> 00:06:44.060
And when it rains, their bodies fill with
water, expanding the matrix and thus the colony

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:50.770
into something we can see and touch and feel
(and yes, sometimes even eat).

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:57.220
So when Dr. Brandes investigated the meadow
for the masses of gelatinous lumps he found

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.130
there, nostoc was a distinct possibility.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:06.240
One apothecary he cited found a Nostoc species
on the ground that sounded similar in some

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:07.259
ways.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:09.530
But his mass was different.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:17.840
It was a very distinct white color, and it
looked more like frog eggs, though not quite.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:24.039
He did what anyone obsessed with a frustrating
identification problem would do and dove into

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:27.190
the literature, coming out with a conclusion:

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:31.250
what he had found was snail spawn.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.130
And who are we to dissuade him?

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:39.160
It sounds like our nostoc weren’t responsible
for the jelly masses he found.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.879
Which is an altogether anticlimactic ending,
we suppose.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:45.130
But we kind of like that.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:51.741
Because over and over again, people find their
version of star jelly, and sometimes it is

00:07:51.741 --> 00:07:53.520
snail spawn.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.630
Sometimes it’s frog spawn that’s been
regurgitated by a bird.

00:07:56.630 --> 00:08:01.070
One time, it even turned out to be waste from
a battery treatment plant.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.060
And every once in a while, it’s even nostoc.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:10.970
Because while the microcosmos isn’t the
answer to everything, it remains a possibility

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:14.650
nonetheless.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:20.000
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.000
us.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.400
The people on the screen right now, they are
our Patreon patrons.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.199
I appreciate them so much and I'm glad to
be making content again and I have also been

00:08:30.199 --> 00:08:33.979
happy to see so many other hosts here on Journey
